Callers use the returned pointer within the function body only (clone scratch, run scans). To safely evict, I can return a `std::shared_ptr<const Regexps>` instead of a raw pointer. The callers bind it as `const auto & hyperscan_regex = MultiRegexps::get<...>(...)` — a const reference to a temporary shared_ptr would extend its lifetime (const ref to temporary extends lifetime). `hyperscan_regex->getScratch()` works on shared_ptr too. So changing return type from `Regexps *` to `std::shared_ptr<Regexps>` is compatible with all four call sites (const auto& to a temporary shared_ptr extends its lifetime — the temporary is the shared_ptr, so it's kept alive).

Plan for minimal bounded cache in Regexps.h:

Replace `Pool` / `RegexpsConstructor` with a bounded cache:

```cpp
/// Bounded cache for compiled vectorscan regexps. Evicts the least recently
/// used entries when the cache grows beyond its capacity instead of expanding
/// indefinitely.
class RegexpsCache
{
public:
    explicit RegexpsCache(size_t max_size_) : max_size(max_size_) {}

    template <bool save_indices, bool WithEditDistance>
    std::shared_ptr<Regexps> getOrCompile(const std::vector<String> & str_patterns, std::optional<UInt32> edit_distance)
    {
        const Key key{str_patterns, edit_distance};

        std::lock_guard lock(mutex);

        auto it = storage.find(key);
        if (it != storage.end())
        {
            /// Move the entry to the back of the LRU list.
            lru.splice(lru.end(), lru, it->second.lru_it);
            return it->second.regexps;
        }

        auto regexps = std::make_shared<Regexps>(constructRegexps<save_indices, WithEditDistance>(str_patterns, edit_distance));

        if (storage.size() >= max_size)
        {
            storage.erase(lru.front());
            lru.pop_front();
        }

        lru.push_back(key);
        storage.emplace(key, Entry{regexps, std::prev(lru.end())});
        return regexps;
    }

private:
    using Key = std::pair<std::vector<String>, std::optional<UInt32>>;

    struct Entry
    {
        std::shared_ptr<Regexps> regexps;
        std::list<Key>::iterator lru_it;
    };

    size_t max_size;
    std::mutex mutex;
    std::list<Key> lru;
    std::map<Key, Entry> storage;
};
```

Hmm, but this changes behavior: previously construction happened outside the global lock (via RegexpsConstructor which locked its own mutex, and emplaced constructor first so concurrent threads wait on per-entry mutex). My version compiles under the global lock — simpler, safe, slight contention change. Acceptable for minimal implementation? It serializes compilation, but compilation is rare (cache misses only). Upstream CacheBase also serializes on insert (it holds a lock during getOrSet? CacheBase::getOrSet uses per-cell... actually it locks whole cache during insertion). Fine.

But wait — exception safety: if constructRegexps throws, nothing inserted — same as before.

Also `std::optional<UInt32>` in Key for map ordering — std::optional has operator<. Previously same key type in std::map. Good.

